<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="notes-Differentiation-Rules-chapter-exercises">
  <title>2. Differentiation Rules- Chapter Exercises</title>

<introduction>
    <p> 
      This section contains exercises covering the foundational differentiation techniques 
      —including the Product Rule, Quotient Rule, Chain Rule, implicit differentiation, logarithmic differentiation
      —and their geometric applications such as related rates problems.
    </p>
  </introduction>
<exercises workplace="Differentiation Rules Exercises">
  
  <!-- EXERCISE 1: ALGEBRAIC DIFFERENTIATION WITH HINTS -->
  <exercise xml:id="ex-diff-skills-1">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Differentiate the following functions:
        <ol marker="(a)">
          <li><p><m>y = x^7 + \sqrt{7}x - \frac{1}{\pi+1}</m></p></li>
          <li><p><m>p = 2\theta\cos\theta</m></p></li>
          <li><p><m>r = \frac{1+\cos\theta}{1-\cos\theta}</m></p></li>
          <li><p><m>y = \sqrt{1-u^2+\tan u}</m></p></li>
          <li><p><m>y = (8-x+3x^2)(2-9x)</m></p></li>
          <li><p><m>y = e^{\sin(x^2)}</m></p></li>
          <li><p><m>y = \ln(1-3x)</m></p></li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <hint>
      <p>
        <ul>
          <li><p>For (a), remember that any term without an explicit variable like <m>x</m> is a constant; its derivative is zero.</p></li>
          <li><p>For (b) and (e), apply the Product Rule: <m>(uv)' = u'v + uv'</m>.</p></li>
          <li><p>For (c), apply the Quotient Rule and simplify the numerator using trig identities.</p></li>
          <li><p>For (d), (f), (g), and (h), apply the Chain Rule: <m>\frac{d}{dx}[f(g(x))] = f'(g(x)) \cdot g'(x)</m>.</p></li>
        </ul>
      </p>
    </hint>
    <solution>
      <p>
        <ol marker="(a)">
          <li>
            <p>
              By the Power Rule, noting that <m>\frac{1}{\pi+1}</m> is a constant:
              <me>\frac{dy}{dx} = 7x^6 + \sqrt{7}</me>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              By the Product Rule <m>(uv)' = u'v + uv'</m>:
              <me>\frac{dp}{d\theta} = 2\cos\theta - 2\theta\sin\theta</me>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              By the Quotient Rule <m>\left(\frac{u}{v}\right)' = \frac{u'v - uv'}{v^2}</m>:
              <me>\frac{dr}{d\theta} = \frac{(-\sin\theta)(1-\cos\theta) - (1+\cos\theta)(\sin\theta)}{(1-\cos\theta)^2}</me>
              <me>\frac{dr}{d\theta} = \frac{-\sin\theta + \sin\theta\cos\theta - \sin\theta - \sin\theta\cos\theta}{(1-\cos\theta)^2} = \frac{-2\sin\theta}{(1-\cos\theta)^2}</me>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              By the Chain Rule:
              <me>\frac{dy}{du} = \frac{1}{2\sqrt{1-u^2+\tan u}} \cdot (-2u + \sec^2 u) = \frac{\sec^2 u - 2u}{2\sqrt{1-u^2+\tan u}}</me>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              By the Product Rule:
              <me>\frac{dy}{dx} = (-1+6x)(2-9x) + (8-x+3x^2)(-9)</me>
              <me>\frac{dy}{dx} = (-2 + 9x + 12x - 54x^2) + (-72 + 9x - 27x^2) = -81x^2 + 30x - 74</me>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              By the Chain Rule (nested twice):
              <me>\frac{dy}{dx} = e^{\sin(x^2)} \cdot \cos(x^2) \cdot 2x = 2x\cos(x^2)e^{\sin(x^2)}</me>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              By the Chain Rule:
              <me>\frac{dy}{dx} = \frac{1}{1-3x} \cdot (-3) = \frac{-3}{1-3x} = \frac{3}{3x-1}</me>
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  <!-- EXERCISE 2: TABULAR DERIVATIVES WITH HINTS -->
  <exercise xml:id="ex-tabular-derivatives">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Suppose that functions <m>f</m> and <m>g</m> and their derivatives with respect to <m>x</m> have the values shown in the table below:
      </p>
      <tabular halign="center" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
        <row header="yes">
          <cell><m>x</m></cell>
          <cell><m>f(x)</m></cell>
          <cell><m>g(x)</m></cell>
          <cell><m>f'(x)</m></cell>
          <cell><m>g'(x)</m></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell>2</cell>
          <cell>8</cell>
          <cell>2</cell>
          <cell><m>1/3</m></cell>
          <cell><m>-3</m></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell>3</cell>
          <cell>3</cell>
          <cell><m>-4</m></cell>
          <cell><m>2\pi</m></cell>
          <cell>5</cell>
        </row>
      </tabular>
      <p>
        Find the value of the following derivatives at the specified points:
        <ol marker="(a)">
          <li><p><m>\dfrac{d}{dx}\left[f(x) - \pi g(x)\right]</m> at <m>x = 3</m></p></li>
          <li><p><m>\dfrac{d}{dx}\left[\sqrt{2}f(x) \cdot g(x)\right]</m> at <m>x = 2</m></p></li>
          <li><p><m>\dfrac{d}{dx}\left[\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}\right]</m> at <m>x = 3</m></p></li>
          <li><p><m>\dfrac{d}{dx}\left[(f \circ g)(x)\right]</m> at <m>x = 2</m></p></li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <hint>
      <p>
        Convert the combinations into formula notations before substituting numbers. For part (d), write the Chain Rule expansion out explicitly: <m>\frac{d}{dx}[f(g(x))] = f'(g(x)) \cdot g'(x)</m>, and find the inner value <m>g(2)</m> first.
      </p>
    </hint>
    <solution>
      <p>
        <ol marker="(a)">
          <li>
            <p>
              The derivative profile is <m>f'(3) - \pi g'(3)</m>.
              Substituting table values: <m>2\pi - \pi(5) = -3\pi</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              By the Product Rule: <m>\sqrt{2}\left(f'(2)g(2) + f(2)g'(2)\right)</m>.
              Substituting values: <m>\sqrt{2}\left((\frac{1}{3})(2) + (8)(-3)\right) = \sqrt{2}\left(\dfrac{2}{3} - 24\right) = -\dfrac{70\sqrt{2}}{3}</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              By the Quotient Rule: <m>\dfrac{f'(3)g(3) - f(3)g'(3)}{(g(3))^2}</m>.
              Substituting values: <m>\dfrac{(2\pi)(-4) - (3)(5)}{(-4)^2} = \dfrac{-8\pi - 15}{16}</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              By the Chain Rule: <m>f'(g(2)) \cdot g'(2)</m>.
              Since <m>g(2) = 2</m>, this evaluates to <m>f'(2) \cdot g'(2)</m>.
              Substituting values: <m>\left(\dfrac{1}{3}\right)(-3) = -1</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  <!-- EXERCISE 3: HIGHER-ORDER DERIVATIVE -->
  <exercise xml:id="ex-second-derivative">
    <statement>
      <p>
        For <m>y = \dfrac{1}{1+x}</m>, find the first and second derivatives.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        Rewrite the expression as <m>y = (1+x)^{-1}</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        First Derivative via Power/Chain Rules:
        <me>\frac{dy}{dx} = -(1+x)^{-2} \cdot (1) = -\frac{1}{(1+x)^2}</me>
        Second Derivative:
        <me>\frac{d^2y}{dx^2} = \frac{d}{dx}\left[-(1+x)^{-2}\right] = 2(1+x)^{-3} \cdot (1) = \frac{2}{(1+x)^3}</me>
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  <!-- EXERCISE 4: IMPLICIT TANGENT SLOPE WITH HINTS -->
  <exercise xml:id="ex-implicit-slope">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Find the slope of the tangent line to the implicitly defined curve <m>x^2y + \sin y = 2\pi</m> at the point <m>P(1, 2\pi)</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <hint>
      <p>
        Differentiate every term with respect to <m>x</m>. Treat <m>y</m> as an implicit function of <m>x</m>, which means appending a <m>\frac{dy}{dx}</m> factor whenever you differentiate a expression containing <m>y</m>. Don't forget the Product Rule on <m>x^2y</m>.
      </p>
    </hint>
    <solution>
      <p>
        Differentiate both sides with respect to <m>x</m>, using the Product Rule on the first term:
        <me>\left(2xy + x^2\frac{dy}{dx}\right) + \cos y \frac{dy}{dx} = 0</me>
        Isolate <m>\dfrac{dy}{dx}</m>:
        <me>\dfrac{dy}{dx}(x^2 + \cos y) = -2xy \implies \frac{dy}{dx} = \frac{-2xy}{x^2 + \cos y}</me>
        Evaluate at <m>x = 1</m> and <m>y = 2\pi</m>, noting that <m>\cos(2\pi) = 1</m>:
        <me>m = \frac{-2(1)(2\pi)}{1^2 + \cos(2\pi)} = \frac{-4\pi}{1 + 1} = -2\pi</me>
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  <!-- EXERCISE 5: LOGARITHMIC DIFFERENTIATION WITH HINTS -->
  <exercise xml:id="ex-log-diff">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Use logarithmic differentiation to find the derivative of <m>y</m> with respect to <m>x</m>:
        <me>y = \dfrac{x\sqrt{x^2+1}}{3(x+1)^{2/3}}</me>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <hint>
      <p>
        Apply the natural log <m>\ln</m> to both sides first. 
        Use the expansion properties: <m>\ln(ab) = \ln a + \ln b</m> and 
        <m>\ln(\dfrac{a}{b}) = \ln a - \ln b</m> to break down the right side into separate, 
        simple logarithms before differentiating.
      </p>
    </hint>
    <solution>
      <p>
        Take the natural logarithm of both sides and expand using log laws:
        <me>\ln y = \ln(x) + \frac{1}{2}\ln(x^2+1) - \ln(3) - \frac{2}{3}\ln(x+1)</me>
        Differentiate implicitly with respect to <m>x</m>:
        <me>\frac{1}{y}\frac{dy}{dx} = \frac{1}{x} + \frac{1}{2}\left(\frac{2x}{x^2+1}\right) - 0 - \frac{2}{3}\left(\frac{1}{x+1}\right)</me>
        <me>\frac{1}{y}\frac{dy}{dx} = \frac{1}{x} + \frac{x}{x^2+1} - \frac{2}{3(x+1)}</me>
        Multiply by <m>y</m> to solve for <m>\dfrac{dy}{dx}</m>:
        <me>\dfrac{dy}{dx} = \left( \frac{x\sqrt{x^2+1}}{3(x+1)^{2/3}} \right) \left[ \frac{1}{x} + \frac{x}{x^2+1} - \frac{2}{3(x+1)} \right]</me>
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

    <!-- EXERCISE 6: INVERSE TRIG DERIVATIVE WITH GEOMETRIC MODEL -->
  <exercise xml:id="ex-arctan-diff">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Differentiate <m>y = \tan^{-1}(x^2+3x)</m> by modeling the expression geometrically as a right triangle using SOHCAHTOA.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <hint>
      <p>
        Let <m>y = \theta</m>. The equation <m>\theta = \tan^{-1}(x^2+3x)</m> means that <m>\tan(\theta) = \dfrac{x^2+3x}{1}</m>. 
        Use the TOA definition (<m>\tan\theta = \frac{\text{Opposite}}{\text{Adjacent}}</m>) to label a right triangle and determine its hypotenuse.
      </p>
    
    
<figure xml:id="fig-inverse-trig-triangle">
  <caption>Geometric right triangle representation of <m>\tan(y) = \frac{x^2+3x}{1}</m>.</caption>
  <image xml:id="img-trig-triangle" width="60%">
    <description>A right triangle with an angle labeled y. The side opposite to y is labeled x^2+3x, the adjacent side is labeled 1, and the hypotenuse is labeled with the square root of 1 plus the quantity x^2+3x squared.</description>
    <latex-image>
      \begin{tikzpicture}[&gt;=stealth, scale=1.3]
          % Draw triangle vertices
          \coordinate (A) at (0,0);
          \coordinate (B) at (4,0);
          \coordinate (C) at (4,2.5);
          
          % Draw the triangle paths with pure LaTeX syntax inside labels
          \draw[thick] (A) -- (B) node[midway, below] {Adjacent = $1$};
          \draw[thick] (B) -- (C) node[midway, right] {Opposite = $x^2+3x$};
          \draw[thick] (C) -- (A) node[midway, sloped, above] {Hypotenuse = $\sqrt{1 + (x^2+3x)^2}$};
          
          % Draw right angle indicator
          \draw (3.8,0) -- (3.8,0.2) -- (4,0.2);
          
          % Draw angle y
          \draw (0.6,0) arc (0:32:0.6);
          \node at (0.8,0.2) {$y$};
      \end{tikzpicture}
    </latex-image>
  </image>
</figure>
</hint>


    <solution>
      <p>
        <alert>Method 1: </alert> To evaluate this using the geometric model, rewrite the inverse equation as a direct trigonometric relationship:
        <me>\tan(y) = \frac{x^2+3x}{1}</me>
        According to SOHCAHTOA, <m>\tan(y) = \frac{\text{Opposite}}{\text{Adjacent}}</m>. 
        We construct a right triangle with an angle <m>y</m>, setting <m>\text{Opposite} = x^2+3x</m> and <m>\text{Adjacent} = 1</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        By the Pythagorean Theorem, the hypotenuse of this triangle is:
        <me>\text{Hypotenuse} = \sqrt{(1)^2 + (x^2+3x)^2} = \sqrt{1 + (x^2+3x)^2}</me>
      </p>
      <p>
        Now, differentiate both sides of <m>\tan(y) = x^2+3x</m> implicitly with respect to <m>x</m>:
        <me>\sec^2(y) \cdot \frac{dy}{dx} = 2x + 3</me>
        Isolate the derivative term:
        <me>\frac{dy}{dx} = \frac{2x+3}{\sec^2(y)} = (2x+3)\cos^2(y)</me>
      </p>
      <p>
        Using our geometric model triangle and the CAH rule (<m>\cos(y) = \frac{\text{Adjacent}}{\text{Hypotenuse}}</m>), read the value of <m>\cos(y)</m> directly from the diagram:
        <me>\cos(y) = \frac{1}{\sqrt{1 + (x^2+3x)^2}}</me>
        Squaring both sides yields:
        <me>\cos^2(y) = \frac{1}{1 + (x^2+3x)^2}</me>
      </p>
      <p>
        Substitute this value back into the isolated derivative equation to reach the final answer:
        <me>\frac{dy}{dx} = (2x+3) \cdot \left(\frac{1}{1 + (x^2+3x)^2}\right) = \frac{2x+3}{1 + x^4 + 6x^3 + 9x^2}</me>
      </p>
      <p>
        <alert>Method 2:</alert> Using the rule <m>\dfrac{d}{dx}[\tan^{-1} u] = \dfrac{1}{1+u^2} \cdot u'</m>:
        <me>\dfrac{dy}{dx} = \dfrac{1}{1 + (x^2+3x)^2} \cdot (2x+3) = \dfrac{2x+3}{1 + x^4 + 6x^3 + 9x^2}</me>
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  <!-- EXERCISE 7: RELATED RATES (POLICE CHASE) WITH HINTS -->
  <exercise xml:id="ex-police-chase">
    <statement>
      <p>
        A police cruiser, approaching a right-angled intersection from the north, is chasing a speeding car that has turned the corner and is now moving straight east. 
        When the cruiser is <m>0.6\text{ mi}</m> north of the intersection and the car is <m>0.8\text{ mi}</m> to the east, the police determine with radar that the distance between them and the car is increasing at <m>20\text{ mph}</m>. 
      </p>
      <p>
        If the cruiser is moving at <m>60\text{ mph}</m> at the instant of measurement, what is the speed of the car?
      </p>
      <figure xml:id="fig-police-chase-geometry">
        <caption>Geometry of the vehicles relative to the right-angle intersection.</caption>
        <image xml:id="img-police-chase" width="60%">
          <description>A right triangle diagram mapping the position of the police cruiser traveling south along the vertical y-axis, and the car moving east along the horizontal x-axis.</description>
          <latex-image>
            \begin{tikzpicture}[&gt;=stealth, scale=3]
                \draw[thick, gray] (0,1) -- (0,0) -- (1.2,0);
                \filldraw[black] (0,0) circle (0.02) node[below left] {Intersection};
                
                \filldraw[blue] (0,0.6) circle (0.03) node[left] {Cruiser ($y$)};
                \draw[-&gt;, blue, thick] (0,0.6) -- (0,0.2) node[midway, left] {$\frac{dy}{dt}$};
                
                \filldraw[red] (0.8,0) circle (0.03) node[below] {Car ($x$)};
                \draw[-&gt;, red, thick] (0.8,0) -- (1.2,0) node[midway, above] {$\frac{dx}{dt}$};
                
                \draw[dashed, purple, thick] (0,0.6) -- (0.8,0) node[midway, above right] {$z$};
            \end{tikzpicture}
          </latex-image>
        </image>
      </figure>
    </statement>
    <hint>
      <p>
        Model this setup as a right triangle where <m>x^2 + y^2 = z^2</m>. 
        Note carefully that because the police cruiser is moving <em>closer</em> 
        to the intersection, its rate of change <m>\dfrac{dy}{dt}</m> must be entered 
        as a negative value (<m>-60</m>).
      </p>
    </hint>
    <solution>
      <p>
        Let <m>y</m> be the distance of the cruiser north of the intersection, <m>x</m> be the distance of the car east of the intersection, and <m>z</m> be the straight-line distance between them. 
        By the Pythagorean Theorem:
        <me>x^2 + y^2 = z^2</me>
      </p>
      <p>
        At the given instant, <m>y = 0.6</m> and <m>x = 0.8</m>. Thus:
        <me>z = \sqrt{0.8^2 + 0.6^2} = \sqrt{0.64 + 0.36} = 1\text{ mi}</me>
        Because the cruiser is traveling south, its distance from the intersection 
        is decreasing, so <m>\dfrac{dy}{dt} = -60\text{ mph}</m>. 
        The radar shows <m>\dfrac{dz}{dt} = 20\text{ mph}</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Differentiating the primary equation implicitly with respect to time <m>t</m>:
        <me>2x\frac{dx}{dt} + 2y\frac{dy}{dt} = 2z\frac{dz}{dt} \implies x\frac{dx}{dt} + y\frac{dy}{dt} = z\frac{dz}{dt}</me>
        Substitute the known parameters to solve for the speed of the car, <m>\dfrac{dx}{dt}</m>:
        <me>(0.8)\frac{dx}{dt} + (0.6)(-60) = (1.0)(20)</me>
        <me>0.8\frac{dx}{dt} - 36 = 20 \implies 0.8\frac{dx}{dt} = 56 \implies \frac{dx}{dt} = \frac{56}{0.8} = 70\text{ mph}</me>
        The speeding car is traveling at <m>70\text{ mph}</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  <!-- EXERCISE 8: PARALLEL TANGENT LINES -->
  <exercise xml:id="ex-parallel-tangent">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Find the points on the graph of <m>y = x^{3/2} - x^{1/2}</m> at which the tangent line is parallel to the line <m>y - x = 3</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        First, identify the required target slope. Rewriting the line <m>y - x = 3</m> as <m>y = x + 3</m> shows its slope is <m>m = 1</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Next, compute the derivative of the target function to represent the general tangent slope:
        <me>\frac{dy}{dx} = \frac{3}{2}x^{1/2} - \frac{1}{2}x^{-1/2} = \frac{3\sqrt{x}}{2} - \frac{1}{2\sqrt{x}}</me>
        Set this derivative equal to the target slope of <m>1</m>:
        <me>\frac{3\sqrt{x}}{2} - \frac{1}{2\sqrt{x}} = 1</me>
        Multiply the entire equation by the common denominator <m>2\sqrt{x}</m> (where <m>x \neq 0</m>):
        <me>3x - 1 = 2\sqrt{x} \implies 3x - 2\sqrt{x} - 1 = 0</me>
        Let <m>u = \sqrt{x}</m>. This converts the relation into a manageable quadratic template:
        <me>3u^2 - 2u - 1 = 0 \implies (3u + 1)(u - 1) = 0</me>
        This yields roots <m>u = -\frac{1}{3}</m> or <m>u = 1</m>. Because <m>u = \sqrt{x} \ge 0</m>, we discard the negative value.
        Thus, <m>\sqrt{x} = 1 \implies x = 1</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Substitute <m>x = 1</m> back into the original curve equation to find the corresponding <m>y</m>-coordinate:
        <me>y = (1)^{3/2} - (1)^{1/2} = 1 - 1 = 0</me>
        The only point on the graph with a tangent parallel to the given line is <m>(1, 0)</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
</exercises>

  <!-- INDEPENDENT REFERENCE DERIVATIVE SOLUTION SHEET -->
  <subsection xml:id="subsec-derivative-reference-sheet">
    <title>Derivative Reference Sheet</title>
    <p>
      Below is the reference guide matching the limit definitions and algebraic rules requested from the chapter footer, using the foundational limit definition of the derivative:
      <me>\frac{dy}{dx} = \lim_{h \to 0} \frac{f(x+h) - f(x)}{h}</me>
    </p>
  
    <table xml:id="table-derivative-limit-definitions">
      <title>The derivative Table</title>
      <caption>Core Functions and Analytical Derivatives</caption>
      <tabular halign="center" top="medium" bottom="medium" left="minor" right="minor">
        <row header="yes" bottom="medium" valign="middle">
        <!-- width="50%" creates perfectly even columns -->
        <!-- valign="middle" ensures perfectly centered rows vertically -->
          <cell width="50%"><m>y = f(x)</m></cell>
          <cell width="50%">Simplified Derivative Formula</cell>
        </row>
        
        <row bottom="minor" valign="middle">
          <cell><m>x^n \vphantom{\dfrac{1}{2}}</m></cell>
          <cell><m>n x^{n-1} \vphantom{\dfrac{1}{2}}</m></cell>
        </row>
        <row bottom="minor" valign="middle">
          <cell><m>\sqrt{x}\vphantom{\dfrac{1}{2}}</m></cell>
          <cell><m>\dfrac{1}{2\sqrt{x}}\vphantom{\dfrac{1}{2}}</m></cell>
        </row>
        <row bottom="minor"  valign="middle">
          <cell><m>\dfrac{1}{x}\vphantom{\dfrac{1}{2}}</m></cell>
          <cell><m>-\dfrac{1}{x^2}\vphantom{\dfrac{1}{2}}</m></cell>
        </row>
        <row bottom="minor"  valign="middle">
          <cell><m>e^x\vphantom{\dfrac{1}{2}}</m></cell>
          <cell><m>e^x\vphantom{\dfrac{1}{2}}</m></cell>
        </row>
        <row bottom="minor"  valign="middle">
          <cell><m>\sin x\vphantom{\dfrac{1}{2}}</m></cell>
          <cell><m>\cos x\vphantom{\dfrac{1}{2}}</m></cell>
        </row>
        <row bottom="minor"  valign="middle">
          <cell><m>\cos x\vphantom{\dfrac{1}{2}}</m></cell>
          <cell><m>-\sin x\vphantom{\dfrac{1}{2}}</m></cell>
        </row>
        <row bottom="minor"  valign="middle">
          <cell><m>\tan x\vphantom{\dfrac{1}{2}}</m></cell>
          <cell><m>\sec^2 x\vphantom{\dfrac{1}{2}}</m></cell>
        </row>
        <row bottom="minor"  valign="middle">
          <cell><m>\sec x\vphantom{\dfrac{1}{2}}</m></cell>
          <cell><m>\sec x \tan x\vphantom{\dfrac{1}{2}}</m></cell>
        </row>
        <row bottom="minor" valign="middle">
          <cell><m>\cot x\vphantom{\dfrac{1}{2}}</m></cell>
          <cell><m>-\csc^2 x\vphantom{\dfrac{1}{2}}</m></cell>
        </row>
        <row bottom="minor"  valign="middle">
          <cell><m>\csc x\vphantom{\dfrac{1}{2}}</m></cell>
          <cell><m>-\csc x \cot x\vphantom{\dfrac{1}{2}}</m></cell>
        </row>
        <row bottom="medium" valign="middle">
          <cell><m>\ln x\vphantom{\dfrac{1}{2}}</m></cell>
          <cell><m>\dfrac{1}{x}\vphantom{\dfrac{1}{2}}</m></cell>
        </row>
      </tabular>
    </table>
  </subsection>





</section>